<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">United States–Mexico–Canada Agreement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2020">2020</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as USMCA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> Agreement (USMCA) is the free trade agreement between the three North American countries that replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-american-free-trade-agreement">NAFTA</a>. Negotiated at the insistence of the Trump administration and signed in 2018, it came into force on 1 July 2020. It tightened rules of origin for cars, added labour and digital-trade provisions and provides for a joint review after six years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-states-mexico-canada-agreement.jpg" alt="United States–Mexico–Canada Agreement" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Addicted04 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:USMCA_on_the_globe_(North_America_centered).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
